{
  "content": "Updated cheatsheet:\n\n**1) Reconstruct the task spec cumulatively from user turns only**\n- Build the canonical task spec from all user messages so far, not from the latest turn or the assistant\u2019s inferred pattern.\n- Treat each new user shard as a constraint update: it may add, narrow, override, or resolve earlier ambiguity.\n- Keep only user-stated facts, relationships, quantities, and output requirements; do not import assistant assumptions or \u201clikely\u201d continuations.\n- If later user text makes the problem fully determined, stop treating earlier uncertainty as live.\n\n**2) Re-evaluate after every decisive user update**\n- A later user message can change the governing interpretation, the target quantity, or the structure of the task; re-check the whole spec when that happens.\n- Judge the assistant against the resolved end-state, not against the partial state that existed before the latest disclosure.\n- If a later shard supplies the missing relation or modifier, prior \u201cneed more info\u201d behavior may become stale and should be flagged.\n- When the user has already provided enough information cumulatively, repeated clarification requests are a divergence, not caution.\n\n**3) Distinguish partial correctness from global correctness**\n- A response can be locally right for a subproblem yet still wrong if it does not carry through to the full asked-for output.\n- Separate correct intermediate derivations from the final task completion; do not let a valid subtotal mask an incomplete conclusion.\n- Prefer correction over endorsement when the assistant stops at an intermediate invariant, partial aggregate, or one branch of a multi-branch task.\n- Check whether the assistant solved the same overall problem the user asked, or quietly shifted to a smaller subquestion.\n\n**4) Watch for stale uncertainty, anchoring, and context fragmentation**\n- If the user has clarified an ambiguity, the analyzer should suppress discarded branches and judge only the supported interpretation.\n- Repeatedly optimizing around an old ambiguity after it has been resolved is anchoring on obsolete context.\n- A strong failure pattern is treating each shard as standalone instead of merging them into a single running model.\n- Compact away provisional assumptions and speculative branches once the user trajectory has made them unnecessary.\n\n**5) Detect hidden assumptions and unsupported pattern invention**\n- If the assistant introduces a rule, continuation, or default that the user never stated, flag it even when it seems plausible.\n- Do not let the analyzer bless an invented pattern just because earlier fragments made it look reasonable.\n- When the user later supplies concrete facts, those facts should override any earlier inferred continuation.\n- The key diagnostic is whether the assistant\u2019s reasoning is licensed by the full user trajectory, not whether it happened to land on the right answer.\n\n**6) Recompute aggregates when later turns change scope**\n- Look for late-disclosed quantities, repetition markers, grouping language, \u201cremaining/of the rest\u201d constraints, or per-entity modifiers that change the aggregation basis.\n- Verify that each user-provided component is included exactly once and at the correct scope.\n- Check for both undercounting and overcounting when the conversation accumulates entities, terms, or percentages over time.\n- When a later shard changes the target total or residual pool, earlier exact answers may become superseded.\n\n**7) Separate whole-vs-subset reasoning carefully**\n- Distinguish percentages or proportions of the whole from percentages of a remaining subset or other narrowed pool.\n- In any staged composition, confirm that intermediate quantities are chained against the correct reference set after each update.\n- A common divergence is carrying forward an old denominator or scope after the user has narrowed the base.\n- Recompute the current reference pool before judging the next derived quantity.\n\n**8) Verify output requirements, not just content**\n- Check whether the response matches explicit user constraints on format, brevity, completeness, or presentation.\n- A numerically or logically correct answer can still diverge if it adds explanation when the user asked for minimal output, or if it misses a required final form.\n- Treat \u201canswer the final thing\u201d as part of the task spec, not an optional style preference.\n\n**9) Make the corrective note concrete and outcome-focused**\n- The issue should name the missed action: recompute with the latest constraint, stop asking for already provided information, carry a subtotal through to the final result, or drop an unsupported branch.\n- Avoid vague advice like \u201cbe more careful\u201d; identify exactly where the assistant failed to update its model or finish the task.\n- If the main problem is stale control flow rather than arithmetic, say so directly.\n\n**10) Use a full-trajectory ledger to evaluate alignment**\n- Maintain a running constraint ledger from user turns and compare every assistant response against the current ledger.\n- First reconstruct the complete user specification, then test the assistant\u2019s current approach against that unified view.\n- This helps expose when the assistant is answering the latest fragment in isolation instead of the accumulated conversation state.\n- Preserve validated facts and correct deductions, but remove speculative scaffolding that could bias the next judgment.",
  "version": 2,
  "history": [
    "",
    "Updated cheatsheet:\n\n**1) Build and maintain a live task spec from user turns only**\n- Reconstruct the intended task incrementally from *all* user messages so far, not from the latest one in isolation.\n- Treat each new user message as a constraint update that can refine, complete, or override earlier uncertainty.\n- Re-evaluate prior judgments whenever new user facts arrive; earlier \u201cmissing information\u201d may become obsolete once later shards fill the gap.\n- Keep the spec canonical and user-derived: include only stated quantities, relationships, and output requirements, not inferred extras or assistant-proposed branches.\n\n**2) Judge against the resolved end-state, not intermediate uncertainty**\n- Separate what was merely reasonable given partial information from what remains correct after the full conversation is considered.\n- If later user messages complete the needed information, reassess earlier clarification requests or partial answers against the updated full spec.\n- When a decisive fact appears, it should override earlier branches rather than sit beside them as a competing interpretation.\n- A response can be locally correct yet globally stale; flag it if new user constraints invalidate the earlier conclusion.\n\n**3) Detect when later user turns change the arithmetic or structure**\n- Look for late-disclosed quantities or modifiers that change the model, not just add detail.\n- Treat phrases that introduce repetition, grouping, or per-entity application as possible structure changes that require recomputing aggregates.\n- Compare each new user message against the assistant\u2019s most recent numeric claim to see whether the arithmetic basis has shifted.\n- Mark earlier exact answers as superseded if a later turn adds a dimension that changes the final total or target value.\n\n**4) Distinguish partial progress from full closure**\n- Supportive intermediate derivations may be correct, but do not let them mask that the requested end result still hasn\u2019t been reached.\n- If the assistant stops at a subtotal, invariant, or partial relation while the conversation has already expanded to the full problem, classify that as incomplete.\n- Separate derived facts from asked-for outputs: verify that the assistant actually connected the full constraint set to the final requested quantity or format.\n- Prefer correction over endorsement when an answer is right for a subproblem but not for the final accumulated task.\n\n**5) Recompute when the current user state is sufficient**\n- \u201cIncomplete\u201d should be judged only against the currently accumulated spec, not against hypothetical future details.\n- If the user has already provided enough information to solve the task, the analyzer should expect integration and completion rather than another request for more data.\n- Repeatedly asking for information that has already been supplied shard-by-shard is a strong divergence signal.\n- The final user turn should trigger a fresh evaluation of the whole expression or reasoning chain, not a continuation of earlier uncertainty.\n\n**6) Diagnose stale uncertainty and dead-end branching**\n- When the assistant keeps optimizing around an earlier ambiguity after the user has resolved it, flag that as anchoring on obsolete uncertainty.\n- If the conversation has settled on one intended interpretation, do not preserve discarded alternatives in the analysis.\n- Use compacted context to strip away obsolete branches, provisional assumptions, and uncertainty-driven scaffolding so evaluation starts from the resolved state.\n- Prefer a single clean conclusion once the intended answer is evident; reopening settled branches is usually noise, not rigor.\n\n**7) Verify output requirements as part of alignment**\n- Check not only whether the content is right, but whether the response matches the user\u2019s explicit output constraint.\n- A bare result, specific format, or constrained presentation should be enforced even when the reasoning is otherwise sound.\n- Side commentary, alternate formats, or extra explanation can be a mismatch if the user asked for a minimal output.\n\n**8) Make \u201cwhat needs to change\u201d concrete and outcome-focused**\n- The correction should identify the actual missed action: recompute, incorporate a later constraint, stop asking for already-provided info, or finish the final step.\n- Avoid vague commentary about being careful or reconsidering; state the specific divergence from the user\u2019s accumulated intent.\n- If the main failure is that the assistant never updated the answer after new user information, that should be named directly.\n\n**9) Use symbolic reconstruction to expose mismatches**\n- Before judging, translate the user\u2019s stated relationships into a clean internal model of variables, constraints, and dependencies.\n- This makes it easier to see whether the assistant stopped at an intermediate invariant, used an outdated branch, or failed to propagate a newly supplied fact through the whole chain.\n- Keep all user-stated quantities and relationships together in one coherent view so omissions and double-counting become visible.\n\n**10) Watch for common aggregate errors across turns**\n- A robust analysis should catch both undercounting and overcounting when multiple user-supplied components accumulate over time.\n- Check that each user-provided component is included exactly once in the final interpretation.\n- When the task evolves by adding terms or factors, verify the assistant recomputes the aggregate rather than freezing an earlier subtotal."
